WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our next guest is a talented
actor you know from

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:05.104
"Flight of the Conchords"
and the "Jumanji" movies.

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:07.206
He stars
in the HBO Max series,

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:08.908
"Our Flag Means Death."

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.377
New episodes are available
to stream Thursdays.

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:12.711
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.711 --> 00:00:15.115
-If it were real, why would she
have sold it to you?

00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:17.684
Why not keep the map herself
and find the treasure herself?

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.786
-Oh, strong question.
Love that.

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:21.320
-Well, maybe she's just lazy.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.554
I mean, she works
down at the docks

00:00:22.554 --> 00:00:24.290
selling maps,
for God's sake.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.825
-Best of luck to you, mate.

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:28.328
I'm taking a nap.

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:30.997
-Last I heard a ship only has
one captain.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.398
And the captain
of this vessel says

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:35.568
"On your feet,
we're having a day."

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:37.403
once I've changed.

00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:40.106
-Please welcome to the show,
Rhys Darby, everybody.

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:41.607
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:48.448 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:55.288 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:57.924
Welcome to the show, Rhys.
-[New Zealand accent] Thank you.

00:00:57.924 --> 00:00:59.658
-I've been a fan of yours for
a very long time.

00:00:59.658 --> 00:01:02.094
And this is, I mean,
it's just an ideal role.

00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:04.230
This is a real pirate?
-It is.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.431
-Stede Bonnet.
-Yes.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.733
So he's on the fringe
of well-known pirates.

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.902
But this one,
it's a crazy story.

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:11.804
So he had a midlife crisis

00:01:11.804 --> 00:01:14.373
and decided to go
and become a pirate.

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:15.875
Now he was an aristocrat.
He had everything.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.310
He was a wealthy landowner.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.845
And to make matters worse,
he left his wife and kids

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:22.582
in the middle of the night
and just took off.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:25.418
-It must be very fun to tell the
story of an unknown

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.288
or lesser-known,
I should say, pirate

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:30.623
and have so much fun
with the comedy side of it.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:33.225
-It is. It's, it's there's
a lot to tackle there,

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:35.061
because why would he do it?
-Yeah.

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:39.232
-And you know, he survived
for a certain amount of time

00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:43.036
without giving too much away.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:45.404
-Still, I don't get the sense.
-All pirates die in the end.

00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:47.440
-Yeah.
-But look, he actually

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.008
purchased the boat, you know.

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:51.911
And he paid his pirates a wage.

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:55.481
And he, he even took obviously
all his fancy clothes with him.

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:58.917
And he even built a library
on his boat.

00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:00.553
It was ridiculous, so.

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:02.788
-You, you mentioned
that he bought a boat.

00:02:02.788 --> 00:02:05.591
You took this very seriously.
You took a sailing lesson.

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:07.726
-Mm-hmm.
-In order to play

00:02:07.726 --> 00:02:09.428
to bring some authenticity
to the pirate.

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:11.864
Did you? Was it helpful to take
a sailing lesson?

00:02:11.864 --> 00:02:14.900
-Well, I got that wrong
because of course,

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.636
this chap had no idea
what he was doing.

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:19.472
And unfortunately, I did.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.108
So I had to double act
once I got here.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.811
Oh, so you made the mistake
of learning a thing

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:26.745
that then you had to pretend
you didn't know how to do?

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:29.381
-I was too qualified. Yes.

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:31.718
That was that was me
going I'll do those ropes.

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.519
Or actually, no, no,
I don't know how to do them.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:34.787
[ Laughter ]

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:37.089
-This is ah, this
is very interesting casting.

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:38.925
A delightful casting.

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:42.094
In the show, we see
a younger version of Stede.

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:45.064
And your son was cast to play
a younger version of you.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:48.934
And here he is with with Taika
there with blood on his face.

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:50.336
[ Audience "Aw's" ]

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.770
-And that's yeah,
that's little Theo.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.472
-Yeah.
-This is was his

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:55.241
first acting role.
-Oh, gotcha.

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.709
And did he take to it?
Did he enjoy it?

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.043
-Oh, he took everything to it.

00:02:58.043 --> 00:03:00.880
And honestly,
hats off to this guy

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.549
because he had to do
his own stunts as well.

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:05.852
The stunt person didn't turn up
or was sick it was --

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:07.620
-Really?
-It was some sort of COVID issue

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.388
or laziness.
Anyway, I'm not sure.

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:12.458
But he was told
he had to run into a tree

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:13.692
so he'd never acted before.

00:03:13.692 --> 00:03:14.961
And also he had to vomit.

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:18.865
So he was fed cold soup by
Taika. He had to do that.

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.701
Run into a tree and then on the
on the boat, in the river,

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:24.570
he was just throwing stones
at, you know,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:25.838
he was literally abused.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:28.007
So it was it was
all part of the role.

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:29.775
But at the end of it, he said,

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:33.279
"Look, I'm not sure
whether I like this as a job,"

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:34.814
and I said, "But you're so
good," I say.

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:37.083
And he said, "Alright, well,
from now on, I'm offer only."

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.384
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.018
So after one job, I don't know
whether that's a record.

00:03:40.018 --> 00:03:42.321
-When you knock it out
of the park on your first job,

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:44.790
you switch to offer only.
-Yeah.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.893
-So much like Stede, as well,
you, you were in the military

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:50.295
in real life.
-I was. Yeah, yeah.

00:03:50.295 --> 00:03:51.697
I mean,
I've been wearing uniform

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:53.700
since I was 12, actually.
-Gotcha.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.768
I did this air training corps
thing,

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:57.970
which in New Zealand
is like a little Air Force thing

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.672
for kids once a week.

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:02.508
And then I switched over
to the, to the Army.

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.943
It was more fun toys
to play with there.

00:04:04.943 --> 00:04:07.246
And, and then I joined the
regular force

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:10.583
at the age of 17
and did this cadet school

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:13.919
and then was a signals operator
for four years.

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:16.256
-Wow. So you were
active military

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:18.557
for four years
and were you someone?

00:04:18.557 --> 00:04:21.293
Did you bring your sense
of humor to that job as well?

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:23.629
-I did, unfortunately.
Yeah.

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:25.798
-Not a place where it's meant to
thrive, yeah.

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:29.035
-They said I'm good for morale,
but if there's an actual war

00:04:29.035 --> 00:04:31.503
about to happen, we're worried
you're gonna ruin things for us.

00:04:31.503 --> 00:04:34.139
-Yeah.
-So they politely let me go,

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:36.275
in the end, they --
it's a funny story.

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:39.645
They actually the C --
the C.O. brought me

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.948
into his office and he said,

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.048
"How do you think
the career is going?"

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:44.984
And I said, "Pretty good, sir."

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:47.486
And he went, "Have a seat.
I've got a pamphlet for you."

00:04:47.486 --> 00:04:49.855
And, and he gave me
this pamphlet.

00:04:49.855 --> 00:04:51.757
It was for a local university.
-Really?

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:53.259
And I said,
"Oh, wow, university!"

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:54.460
He said,
"That's be good for you."

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.962
He said, "You know, with your
English skills

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:58.964
and you're a good computer--
communicator."

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:02.535
I can't even say the word.
He got that wrong.

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:06.338
But I said, "Well, will I have
time off to do university."

00:05:06.338 --> 00:05:09.308
And he said, "Oh yeah,
I think we'll give you,

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.310
I think we'll give you
a lot of time off."

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.312
-So this is interesting
because I think historically

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:16.215
people think of the military
and maybe setting up a booth

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:19.018
at a university
handing out pamphlets.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:20.987
But this was you were
so unique

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:23.088
that they sent you
the other way.

00:05:23.088 --> 00:05:25.024
-I'm one of the only ones
that was sent the other way.

00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:27.259
I think, that yeah.

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:28.928
Which yeah,
it was the right move.

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:32.464
-They de-cruited you.
-Yeah, yeah. Yeah, it was.

00:05:32.464 --> 00:05:33.866
It was a soft let go.

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:35.434
But I did manage
to keep the jacket.

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:36.969
-You -- Oh, they
let you keep your jacket?

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.872
-Yeah. I wanted all the kit, but
they said, No, we need that."

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.006
But, um,
you can take the jacket.

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:42.274
-Well, I think if you have
the full kit.

00:05:42.274 --> 00:05:43.976
You can pretend
like you're still in the Army.

00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:45.911
But if you just have the jacket,
people know your foolin'.

00:05:45.911 --> 00:05:47.080
-That's it. Yeah. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:48.814
But you -- signals operator.

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:50.950
So you learned Morse code?

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:52.318
-Yes.
And is this something?

00:05:52.318 --> 00:05:54.887
Do you forget Morse code
once you stop using it?

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:55.955
-Over time.
-Yeah.

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:57.389
-Absolutely.

00:05:57.389 --> 00:05:58.891
But I've sort of stuck with it.

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:01.160
-Okay.
-It's a handy thing to have.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.962
-I mean, it comes so in handy.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:03.962 --> 00:06:07.099
-I look honestly, we were
the last of the Morse coders.

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:08.935
It's not even trained anymore.
-Really?

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.068
-Yeah.
-Well, I mean,

00:06:10.068 --> 00:06:11.504
I guess that makes sense.
I'm surprised that

00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:13.973
you were even in the generation
where they were using it.

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:15.340
-Well, this was New Zealand.
-Oh.

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:18.010
-So you know, I mean,
they stopped doing

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.412
Morse code last year.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:20.412 --> 00:06:23.316
-You know, it was an intensive
course like three months,

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:25.318
just hearing all the dots
and dashes

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:26.753
and you're writing stuff down,
you know,

00:06:26.753 --> 00:06:29.555
and then my big job was all
these codes would come through

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:32.724
and I'd decode them
and then hand it to the officer.

00:06:32.724 --> 00:06:34.260
And, you know,
the battle would commence.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.262
-Could you still could you
still understand

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:38.030
or even could you speak
Morse code?

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.799
-Uh, yes, I could, certainly.
-Could you say something

00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:43.769
in Morse code now?
-Okay. Here we go.

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:52.478
[ Imitating Morse code ]

00:06:56.081 --> 00:06:57.183
-Oh, wow.
-Yeah.

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:58.251
-That was great.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:00.786
-What uh?

00:07:00.786 --> 00:07:03.588
I mean, are you --
do you want to tell us

00:07:03.588 --> 00:07:05.391
what you said or should we just
wait until somebody

00:07:05.391 --> 00:07:08.160
can, you know?
-Well, be funny the way.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.494
I was going to tell you
it was, uh...

00:07:09.494 --> 00:07:12.264
-What was it?
-"I wish I was somewhere else."

00:07:12.264 --> 00:07:15.434
But it wasn't that! It was
"Seth rocks." There you go.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:19.605
-[ Laughs ] "Seth rocks."
-You guys, that's Rhys Darby.

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:23.309
New episode of "Our Flag
Means Death" are available to

00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:25.277
stream Thursdays on HBO Max.

00:07:25.277 --> 00:07:26.546
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

